-1.27 1-2.09C10 5.22 8.98 4 8 4H4c-.98 0-2 1.22-2 2.5S3 9 4 9zm9-3h-1v1h1c1 0 2 1.22 2 2.5S13.98 12 13 12H9c-.98 0-2-1.22-2-2.5 0-.83.42-1.64 1-2.09V6.25c-1.09.53-2 1.84-2 3.25C6 11.31 7.55 13 9 13h4c1.45 0 3-1.69 3-3.5S14.5 6 13 6z"},"children":[]}]}]},{"type":"text","value":"The agent matters more than anything else I've tested"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The two agents that "},{"type":"element","tagName":"em","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"did"}]},{"type":"text","value":" work are interesting.\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"https://github.com/SWE-agent/mini-swe-agent"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"mini-swe-agent"}]},{"type":"text","value":" resolved 308/500\n(61.6%) against openhands' 274/500 (54.8%)."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"That is a 6.8pp swing from changing nothing but the scaffold, and it is the\nlargest effect of any single knob I've tested in this entire series.  For\ncomparison, the difference between BF16, Q8_0, and Q5_K_M is usually a couple of\npoints, and Part 5 showed that sampling presets barely register at all.  61.6%\nis also the best result I've gotten across all six posts.  The previous best was\nthe 57.6% from the vllm run above."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is worth holding next to Qwen's claimed 70.0% on SWE-bench Verified.  Their\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"https://qwen.ai/blog?id=qwen3.6-35b-a3b"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"footnote"}]},{"type":"text","value":" says they used an \"internal\nagent scaffold (bash + file-edit tools),\" which is a much closer fit to\nmini-swe-agent's minimal design than of openhands."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"h1","properties":{"id":"concluding-thoughts","style":"position:relative;"},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"#concluding-thoughts","ariaLabel":"concluding thoughts permalink","className":["anchor","before"]},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"svg","properties":{"ariaHidden":"true","focusable":"false","height":"16","version":"1.1","viewBox":"0 0 16 16","width":"16"},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"path","properties":{"fillRule":"evenodd","d":"M4 9h1v1H4c-1.5 0-3-1.69-3-3.5S2.55 3 4 3h4c1.45 0 3 1.69 3 3.5 0 1.41-.91 2.72-2 3.25V8.59c.58-.45 1-1.27 1-2.09C10 5.22 8.98 4 8 4H4c-.98 0-2 1.22-2 2.5S3 9 4 9zm9-3h-1v1h1c1 0 2 1.22 2 2.5S13.98 12 13 12H9c-.98 0-2-1.22-2-2.5 0-.83.42-1.64 1-2.09V6.25c-1.09.53-2 1.84-2 3.25C6 11.31 7.55 13 9 13h4c1.45 0 3-1.69 3-3.5S14.5 6 13 6z"},"children":[]}]}]},{"type":"text","value":"Concluding Thoughts"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The llama.cpp/vllm discrepancy from Part 5 was a llama.cpp bug, and upgrading\nfixes it.  The thinking penalty itself is still there on both backends and I\nstill don't know why."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"h2","properties":{"id":"next-steps","style":"position:relative;"},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"#next-steps","ariaLabel":"next steps permalink","className":["anchor","before"]},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"svg","properties":{"ariaHidden":"true","focusable":"false","height":"16","version":"1.1","viewBox":"0 0 16 16","width":"16"},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"path","properties":{"fillRule":"evenodd","d":"M4 9h1v1H4c-1.5 0-3-1.69-3-3.5S2.55 3 4 3h4c1.45 0 3 1.69 3 3.5 0 1.41-.91 2.72-2 3.25V8.59c.58-.45 1-1.27 1-2.09C10 5.22 8.98 4 8 4H4c-.98 0-2 1.22-2 2.5S3 9 4 9zm9-3h-1v1h1c1 0 2 1.22 2 2.5S13.98 12 13 12H9c-.98 0-2-1.22-2-2.5 0-.83.42-1.64 1-2.09V6.25c-1.09.53-2 1.84-2 3.25C6 11.31 7.55 13 9 13h4c1.45 0 3-1.69 3-3.5S14.5 6 13 6z"},"children":[]}]}]},{"type":"text","value":"Next Steps"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"I've now spent five posts on Qwen3.5-35B-A3B.  It's time to point the harness at other models:"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"ol","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"li","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Qwen3.5-27B, the dense model I've been meaning to test since Part 4."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"li","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Newer Qwen3.6 and Qwen3.8 variants. Qwen3.6-35B-A3B is an obvious one.  Qwen3.8 doesn't have any similarly sized MoE models, so Qwen3.8-27B is probably the next best option."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"li","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Gemma 4.  "},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"/blog/2026-05-15--commonsense-benchmarks-for-local-coding-agents/"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Part\n1"}]},{"type":"text","value":" opened\nwith quantized Gemma 4 being completely unusable for coding agents, which is\nwhat motivated this whole project.  It seems only fair to give it a proper\nmeasurement."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"}]}],"data":{"quirksMode":false}},"excerpt":"In Part\n1 and Part\n2,\nI reported some peculiar behavior for quantized Qwen3.5-2B models.  In Part\n3\nI moved to Qwen3.5-35B-A3B.  Part…","timeToRead":4,"fields":{"slug":"/blog/2026-09-04--benchmarking-quantized-ll-ms-for-local-coding-agents-part-6-one-mystery-solved/"},"frontmatter":{"tags":["llm","ai","autobench"],"author":{"jsonId":"Edward J. Schwartz","bio":"Computer Security Researcher","twitter":"@edmcman","avatar":{"childImageSharp":{"gatsbyImageData":{"layout":"fixed","placeholder":{"fallback":"data:image/png;base64,"},"imag